    "requirements": [
      {
        "requirement": "A single hex base",
        "status": "partial",
        "evidence": "One base, printed as a single fused part: a 3-storey north-half base at 0,0 (piece:0,0,0..2;half-n with fused joins). It's a half base rather than a full one, because a full base takes no drawers. It's also three storeys tall, which you may count as stacking."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Nothing else added beside or on top of it",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "The only parts are the base and its drawers. There are no neighbouring cells, covers, caps, bolts or other parts."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Five drawers in it",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "Only 3 drawers fit (drawer:0#0,0#cut, drawer:1#0,0#cut, drawer:2#0,0#cut). A half base takes one drawer per storey, and 3 storeys is the most a base can have. Full and quarter bases take none."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "Five drawers in one hex base isn't possible here. Drawers only fit on the cut face of a half base, and each storey has one cut face, so each storey takes one drawer. A full base offers no drawer spot at all, and a quarter base doesn't either. The tallest single base the configurator allows is a 3-storey half base, made as one fused part, so the most I could fit is 3 drawers. That's what I built: a 3-storey north-half base at cell 0,0 with one standard drawer in each storey (drawer:0, drawer:1 and drawer:2). The configurator's checks found no rule errors and no parts hitting each other. I didn't add end caps or bolts, the usual finishing steps, because you asked for nothing beside or on top of the base. Nine open faces could still take caps if you want them later. Getting five drawers would mean stacking or adding more bases, which goes against your brief.",
    "outcome": "compromise"
  },
